===============================================================================
Date: 2026-11-02
Author: Antigravity (6f28647a-39a6-4a0a-8303-30b57a122458)
Scope: Resolution of `work/socket-lifetime-proposal-25/proposal.txt` and its 3 open integration conditions prior to implementation in `JPIPTCPSocket.swift`, `JPIPServer.swift`, and `JPIPWebSocketServer.swift`.

1. Core Descriptor State Machine (`JPIPSocketDescriptor`)
---------------------------------------------------------
- Each published `JPIPTCPListener` and `JPIPTCPConnection` owns one immutable `JPIPSocketDescriptor` instance for its file descriptor. A descriptor number is never reopened or reassigned inside the same owner.
- Guarded by a single short `NSLock`, `JPIPSocketDescriptor` tracks:
  - `fd: Int32` (valid only while owned; cleared to `-1` when the unique physical-close obligation is claimed)
  - `admissionOpen: Bool` (`true` initially; set to `false` atomically on first `initiateClose()` or terminal listener error)
  - `activeBorrows: Int` (count of active I/O, poll/accept, timeout-setter, and control-shutdown borrowers)
  - `shutdownClaimed: Bool` (ensures connected-socket `shutdown(SHUT_RDWR)` is claimed at most once)
  - `physicalCloseClaimed: Bool` (ensures `close(fd)` is claimed at most once, only when `!admissionOpen && activeBorrows == 0`)
  - `terminalResult: Result<Void, J2KError>?` (`nil` until physical `close(fd)` returns; `.success(())` when `close(fd) == 0`, or `.failure(J2KError.networkError(...))` with preserved `closeErrno: Int32` when `close(fd) != 0`)
  - `waiters: [CheckedContinuation<Result<Void, J2KError>, Never>]` (resumed outside all locks after `terminalResult` is recorded)
- Physical `close(fd)` is NEVER retried on `EINTR` or any other error, preventing accidental closure of a recycled descriptor number.
- Connected-socket `close()` atomically sets `admissionOpen = false` and claims a single control borrower (`activeBorrows += 1`, `shutdownClaimed = true`) while `fd >= 0`. Outside the state lock, it invokes `shutdown(fd, SHUT_RDWR)` and then releases the control borrower. Physical `close(fd)` can only occur after the control borrower AND all I/O and `setsockopt` borrowers have released.
- Listener `close()` atomically sets `admissionOpen = false` without calling `shutdown` on the listening socket; if `activeBorrows == 0`, it claims physical `close(fd)` immediately; otherwise the in-flight `acceptConnection` borrower claims physical `close(fd)` when its `50ms` `poll` / non-blocking `accept` scope exits.

2. Resolution of the 3 Open Integration Conditions
--------------------------------------------------
Condition 1 — No `readLock`, `writeLock`, or `wsReadLock` held across physical close or completion callbacks:
- `JPIPSocketDescriptor.lock` is a pure leaf lock (acquired and released inside `acquireBorrow`, `initiateClose`, and `release`).
- Release order is strictly `operationLock.unlock() -> borrow.release()`:
  - In every connection operation (`writeAll`, `readExact`, `readToEOFForTesting`, `readHTTPRequest`, `readHTTPResponse`, `readWebSocketFrame`), `let borrow = try descriptor.acquireBorrow(operation: ...)` and `defer { borrow.release() }` are registered BEFORE `lockTracker.enter(opLock)` and `defer { lockTracker.exit(opLock) }`.
  - Because Swift `defer` blocks run in LIFO order, `lockTracker.exit(opLock)` ALWAYS unlocks `opLock` and decrements the active operation-lock count to `0` before `borrow.release()` runs.
  - Because `descriptor.acquireBorrow` is called before `lockTracker.enter`, once `close()` retires the descriptor no new caller can enter `lockTracker.enter`.
  - `JPIPOperationLockTracker` increments the counter before `opLock.lock()` and decrements it after `opLock.unlock()`, allowing `JPIPSocketDescriptor` to assert and report `heldOperationLocks == 0` at `.beforePhysicalClose`, `.afterPhysicalClose`, and `.beforeWaiterResume`.

Condition 2 — Prevent accept-loop busy spin on persistent `poll`/`accept` errors on a still-open listener:
- During `JPIPTCPListener.init`, `O_NONBLOCK` is set on the listening descriptor via checked `fcntl(F_GETFL)` / `fcntl(F_SETFL)` preserving existing flags.
- `acceptConnection(timeoutSeconds:)` acquires one descriptor borrow for the entire `poll`/`accept`/publication scope and polls `POLLIN` with a finite `50ms` wait quantum.
- Transient conditions (`poll == 0` timeout, `EINTR` after rechecking `!borrow.isRetired`, `accept` returning `EAGAIN`/`EWOULDBLOCK` from stale readiness, or `ECONNABORTED` from an aborted pending connection) return to the `50ms` `poll` loop after checking `!borrow.isRetired`.
- Any unexpected/persistent `poll` or `accept` error (including `POLLNVAL`, `POLLERR`/`POLLHUP` without `POLLIN`, `EBADF`, `EINVAL`, `ENOTSOCK`, `EOPNOTSUPP`, `EMFILE`, `ENFILE`, `ENOBUFS`, `ENOMEM`, or accepted-socket `F_GETFL`/`F_SETFL` failure) atomically retires listener admission (`admissionOpen = false`) BEFORE throwing `J2KError.networkError(...)`.
  - For `POLLNVAL` while owner-open, admission is retired and `terminalResult` is recorded as an invariant error (`EBADF`) without issuing `close(fd)` on an invalid descriptor.
  - Because `listener.isOpen` becomes `false` before `acceptConnection` throws, any caller loop (`while tcpListener.isOpen` in `JPIPServer.start` or test peers) terminates immediately and cannot busy-spin.
- For a valid accepted `clientFD >= 0`, `O_NONBLOCK` is explicitly cleared via checked `F_GETFL`/`F_SETFL` preserving other flags, socket options (`TCP_NODELAY`, `SO_NOSIGPIPE`, `SO_RCVTIMEO`, `SO_SNDTIMEO`) are configured, and listener retirement is rechecked atomically at the publication point (`admitAcceptedPublication()`). If the listener retired before publication, the unexposed `clientFD` is closed once and `acceptConnection` returns `nil`.

Condition 3 (Updated per Codex Architecture Feedback #2) — Explicit `idle` / `starting(generation)` / `running(generation)` / `stopping(generation)` lifecycle state machine and generation-bound ownership in `JPIPServer` and `JPIPWebSocketServer`:
- Both `JPIPServer` and `JPIPWebSocketServer` maintain an explicit `LifecyclePhase` state machine (`.idle`, `.starting(generation: UInt64)`, `.running(generation: UInt64)`, `.stopping(generation: UInt64)`) with a monotonically increasing `lifecycleGeneration: UInt64`:
  1. `start()` requires `lifecyclePhase == .idle`; if `.starting`, `.running`, or `.stopping`, `start()` immediately throws `J2KError.invalidState(...)` before touching any listener, queue, or delegate. This prevents both `start/start` and `stop/start` reentrancy races across `await` points.
  2. `start()` synchronously advances `lifecycleGeneration &+= 1` and sets `lifecyclePhase = .starting(generation)` before the first `await`. On failure, `start()` restores `lifecyclePhase = .idle` (and in `JPIPWebSocketServer`, clears the delegate only `ifMatchingGeneration: generation`). On success, `start()` transitions to `lifecyclePhase = .running(generation)` with a fresh per-generation `JPIPRequestQueue`.
  3. `stop()` requires `case .running(let generation) = lifecyclePhase`, synchronously sets `lifecyclePhase = .stopping(generation)` and `isRunning = false` before the first `await`, and uses `defer { lifecyclePhase = .idle; isRunning = false }` so the server remains in `.stopping(generation)` across all awaits (`session.close()`, detached `retiringQueue.clear()`, `connection.close()`, `clearWebSocketDelegate(ifMatchingGeneration:)`, and `retiringListener.waitForCloseCompletion()` / `jpipServer.stop()`), and always transitions cleanly to `.idle` even if close-completion throws an error.
  4. All worker callbacks and actor entry points (`serveClientConnection`, `handleWireHTTPRequest`, `handleRequest`, `closeSession`, `serveWebSocketUpgrade`, `getWebSocketDelegate(forServerGeneration:)`, `acceptLiveSocketUpgrade`, `handleUpgradeRequest`, `handleFrame`, `closeConnection`, `performHealthCheck`, `sendKeepalivePings`, `pushDataBin`) validate the active generation before and after suspension points so delayed callbacks from an older generation cannot mutate a stopping or restarted server generation.
  5. Cross-actor generation ownership (`Codex Feedback #3` `P1`):
     - `JPIPServer` and `JPIPWebSocketServer` maintain independent generation counters that can diverge if one actor fails during `start()`. Neither actor ever assumes numeric equality (`wsServer.lifecycleGeneration == jpipServer.lifecycleGeneration`).
     - `JPIPServer.getWebSocketDelegate(forServerGeneration:)` returns `(isActive, delegate, delegateGeneration, hopHook)` and `JPIPServer.serveWebSocketUpgrade` passes `expectedGeneration: delegateGen` into `wsServer.acceptLiveSocketUpgrade(headers:socket:expectedGeneration:)`, which validates `expectedGeneration` before mutating `statistics`, `connections`, or `liveSockets`.
     - `JPIPWebSocketServer.start()` captures `jpipServer.runningGeneration` into `activeBackendGeneration` and records `connectionBackendGenerations[connectionID]` upon upgrade. Every `JPIPWebSocketServer -> JPIPServer` call (`handleRequest(request, expectedGeneration: backendGeneration)` and `closeSession(sessionID, expectedGeneration: backendGeneration)` across `handleFrame`, `handleRequestFrame`, `handleControlFrame`, `performHealthCheck`, and `closeConnection`) passes the captured backend generation token and re-verifies `activeBackendGeneration == backendGeneration` after suspension points.
     - `JPIPServer.getOrCreateSession(for:)` is synchronous so no suspension point exists between generation validation and session insertion.
  6. Pending native kernel I/O cancellation proof (`Codex Feedback #3` `P2`):
     - `T7` keeps borrow-before-I/O cancellation coverage (`3A`, via `.afterConnectionBorrowAcquired`) explicitly separate from pending native kernel I/O cancellation coverage (`3B` and `4`).
     - In `3B` (`readExact(count: 64)` with `30s` `SO_RCVTIMEO`), `serverConn` writes `32` bytes first (`POLLIN == 1`), `clientConn.readExact(count: 64)` runs on a dedicated worker, the test waits until `poll(POLLIN)` clears to `0` (proving the first native `recv()` drained the 32 bytes and `readExact` immediately looped into the second blocking native `recv()` syscall for the remaining 32 bytes), verifies the worker stays blocked for `50ms`, calls `recvToken.cancel()`, and asserts completion in `< 1.0s` with a non-timeout error.
     - In `4` (`writeAll(16 MiB)` with `30s` `SO_SNDTIMEO`), the test waits until `poll(POLLIN) == 1` on `idlePeer` (proving native `send()` syscalls delivered bytes into the kernel loopback) and `poll(POLLOUT) == 0` on `sendClient` (proving `sendClient`'s kernel send buffer is saturated and the current native `send()` syscall is blocked in the kernel), verifies the worker stays blocked for `50ms`, calls `sendToken.cancel()`, and asserts completion in `< 1.0s` with a non-timeout error.
  7. Test-only assertion strengthening and comment qualification (`Codex Feedback #4`):
     - Production sources (`Sources/SwiftJ2KJPIP/JPIPTCPSocket.swift`, `JPIPServer.swift`, `JPIPWebSocketServer.swift`) are 100% unchanged from `candidate-snapshot-26`.
     - In `T8` (`8D(2)`), the test joins both the resumed server-side `serveWebSocketUpgrade` worker (via a reference-counted `UpgradeWorkerCompletionBeacon` attached to the accepted server connection's `syscallSeam` that signals only after `serveWebSocketUpgrade` completes `acceptLiveSocketUpgrade` and exits its worker frame) and a direct server-side `acceptLiveSocketUpgrade(headers:socket:expectedGeneration:)` hop task, asserting the returned `UpgradeResult` (`!isSuccess`, `connectionID == nil`, `generation == nil`, `error == "Server is not running"`) and unchanged admission counters (`statistics.upgradeRequests`, `statistics.failedUpgrades`, `statistics.activeConnections == 0`, `getActiveConnectionCount() == 0`).
     - In `T8` (`8D(3a)` and `8D(3b)`), delayed stale `cnew` and delayed stale `cclose=*` are tested in separate restart cycles (`3a` proving `0 -> 0` sessions with no phantom creation; `3b` proving `1 -> 1` sessions and verifying the exact surviving successor session identity `succ-active-session` via a stateful `cid = succ-active-session` request alongside rejection of a non-existent `cid`).
     - In `T7`, comments around `3B` and `4` qualify that readiness, partial byte drain/backpressure saturation, 50ms non-completion, and `< 1.0s` cancellation completion against a `30s` socket timeout are directly observed, from which blocking in the subsequent native `recv()`/`send()` call is inferred.
